// Ore processing's handbook texts that quote the pack's figures (lang/en.json), each with {{name}}
// where a figure goes. make_ore_guide_numbers.py (beside this) fills them from config/ore-processing.json
// and a few C# constants and writes them into en.json; its figures() names every placeholder.
// Edit these texts here, never in en.json, then run
//   python3 mods-src/seraphhorizons/Ore/tools/make_ore_guide_numbers.py
// tools/tests/test_ore_guide_numbers.py fails CI when en.json and this drift apart.
{
  "oreprocessing-rawore-sulfide-handbook-text": "A sulfide: its metal is bound to sulfur, and it does not smelt as it is. Roast it in a firepit on any fuel, at about {{roastPoint}} °C, into roasted ore, which smelts at its grade's share less what the fire loses (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreroasting\">Roasting sulfide ore</a>); or crush and concentrate it, which keeps far more (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>). Set it down on the ground and strike it with a hammer to break it into crushed ore (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-spalling\">Spalling ore</a>).",
  "oreprocessing-guide-text": "Ore comes out of the ground as rock with metal in it, and every step from the rock face to the ingot either keeps that metal or loses some of it. This page is the whole line; each item's own page says what that item is for, and the guides linked below go into the steps. The figures are the pack's defaults.\n\n<strong>Grades, and smelting rock</strong>\nRaw ore holds metal by its grade: most ores {{rawUnits}} units a piece, poor to bountiful. A furnace has to slag the rock off, and metal goes with the slag, so rock (raw, crushed or ground ore alike) smelts at only a share of what it holds. A sulfide's roasted ore smelts at that share times what its roast kept:\n\n<strong>Poor</strong>: {{smelt.poor}} %; roasted in a firepit {{roasted.poor}} %, in the stall roaster {{stallRoasted.poor}} %\n<strong>Medium</strong>: {{smelt.medium}} %; roasted {{roasted.medium}} %, stall-roasted {{stallRoasted.medium}} %\n<strong>Rich</strong>: {{smelt.rich}} %; roasted {{roasted.rich}} %, stall-roasted {{stallRoasted.rich}} %\n<strong>Bountiful</strong>: {{smelt.bountiful}} %; roasted {{roasted.bountiful}} %, stall-roasted {{stallRoasted.bountiful}} %\n\nConcentrate, roasted concentrate and retorted sponge smelt whole: {{smeltWhole}} %. So concentrating is the step that keeps the metal: it throws the rock away before the furnace sees it.\n\n<strong>Crushing and grinding</strong>\nCrushing never loses metal: one raw ore breaks into one crushed ore of its grade (crystallised ore into two). By hand, set raw ore on the ground and strike it with a hammer (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-spalling\">Spalling ore</a>); the jaw breaker, the stamp battery and the pulverizer crush by power. Crushed ore smelts as the raw ore does; its worth is that the concentrators take it. Poor ore is fine-grained: grind it into ground ore on the arrastra or the ball mill, or the concentrators catch little of it. The quern grinds no ore.\n\n<strong>Concentrating</strong>\nThe gold pan and the rocker by hand, and the sluice, jig, shaking table and vanner by water and power, wash the light rock away and keep the heavy grains as <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:concentrate-hematite\">concentrate</a>, {{concentrateUnits}} units of metal a piece. By hand the pan keeps {{pan}} % and the rocker {{rocker}} % of what they can catch, and what they miss is spent. The machines each catch their share of what the one before missed (the sluice {{sluice}} %, the jig {{jig}} %, the table {{table}} %, the vanner {{vanner}} %), so a full line keeps nearly all of it. Feed that was not classified, poor ore that was not ground and free gold or silver that was not amalgamated hold part of their metal out of reach.\n\n<strong>Roasting</strong>\nGalena, chalcopyrite, sphalerite and the other sulfides do not smelt as they are, raw, crushed, ground or concentrated: they are roasted first, which burns the sulfur off.\nIn a <a href=\"handbook://block-firepit-cold\">firepit</a>, on any fuel, at about {{roastPoint}} °C, raw and crushed sulfide ore roast one for one into roasted ore, and concentrate into <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:roastedconcentrate-galena\">roasted concentrate</a>. Open roasting keeps {{firepit}} % of the metal: 20 concentrate roast to {{firepit.of20}}, and roasted ore smelts at its grade's share times {{firepit}} %.\nThe stall roaster takes a heap of raw or crushed rock at a time: stall-roasted ore smelts at its grade's share times {{stallroaster}} %, and {{stallroaster.sulfur}} of the sulfur comes back as sulfur powder.\nThe reverberatory roaster takes concentrate by chute and {{reverberatory.loses}}; {{reverberatory.sulfur}} % of the sulfur comes back.\nGround sulfide ore does not roast as rock: concentrate it first. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreroasting\">Roasting sulfide ore</a>.\n\n<strong>Amalgam and the still</strong>\nGold and silver quartz carry free metal that gravity alone partly misses. Mercury takes it up: the amalgam pan and the amalgamation plates catch it as <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:amalgam-quartz_nativegold\">amalgam</a>, which does not smelt. Retort the amalgam in the still (a boiler on a lit firepit with a condenser beside it): {{mercuryReturn}} of its mercury comes back, and the gold or silver stays behind as retorted sponge, which smelts whole. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreretorting\">Retorting mercury</a>.\n\n<strong>Bullion and parting</strong>\nSome ores carry a second metal, and every form of them smelts to <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:bullion-galena-lead\">base bullion</a>: a bar of {{barUnits}} units of the first metal with the second still in it.\n<strong>Galena, argentiferous galena</strong>: silver-lead ({{byProduct.galena}} or {{byProduct.galena_nativesilver}} silver), parted in the cupellation furnace\n<strong>Tetrahedrite</strong>: argentiferous copper ({{byProduct.tetrahedrite}} silver), in the cupellation furnace with {{cupel.leadAdded}} lead added\n<strong>Freibergite</strong>: cupriferous silver ({{byProduct.freibergite}} copper), in the cupellation furnace with {{cupel.leadAdded}} lead added\n<strong>Teallite, franckeite</strong>: tin-lead ({{byProduct.teallite}} or {{byProduct.franckeite}} lead), in the liquation furnace\n<strong>Gold quartz</strong>: doré ({{byProduct.quartz_nativegold}} silver), in the acid parting vessel\nThe parting machines win the second metal at {{partingMachines}} %. Smelted again as it is, a bar gives its first metal (doré {{unparted.quartz_nativegold}} % of its gold) and the second is lost. By hand, the <a href=\"handbook://block-seraphhorizons:cupel-fired\">cupel</a> parts the silver from roasted galena, tetrahedrite or freibergite concentrate, and the liquation pan the lead from roasted teallite or franckeite concentrate, at {{handParting}} % (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-liquation\">Liquation: tin from lead</a>).\n\n<strong>Rich lead and litharge</strong>\nPlain galena's silver-lead can go through the Parkes kettle first, which takes its silver out with zinc into <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:richlead\">rich lead</a>, lead {{richLeadSilver}} of which is silver; the cupellation furnace parts all of that silver. Cupellation burns the lead to <a href=\"handbook://item-seraphhorizons:litharge\">litharge</a>, which smelts back to lead at a small loss, {{litharge.out}} to every {{litharge.in}}.",
  "oreproduct-crushed-sulfide-handbook-text": "A sulfide's crushed ore does not smelt as it is. Roast it in a firepit at about {{roastPoint}} °C, or in the stall roaster, into roasted ore that smelts at its grade's share less what the roast loses (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreroasting\">Roasting sulfide ore</a>); or concentrate it and roast the concentrate, which keeps far more. Poor ore is fine-grained: grind it before the concentrators. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-roastedrock-handbook-text": "A sulfide's raw or crushed ore roasted in a firepit, its sulfur burned off. It smelts at its grade's share times the firepit's {{firepit}} %: open roasting loses the rest with the fumes, and a slot of rock is still a slot of rock, so concentrating first keeps far more. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreroasting\">Roasting sulfide ore</a> and <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-stallroastedrock-handbook-text": "A sulfide's raw or crushed ore roasted in the stall roaster, its sulfur burned off. It smelts at its grade's share times the stall's {{stallroaster}} %, where the firepit's roasted ore keeps {{firepit}} %. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-concentrate-handbook-text": "The heavy grains a concentrator keeps, the rock thrown away: {{concentrateUnits}} units of metal a piece, which smelt whole in any furnace. A sulfide's concentrate (galena, chalcopyrite, sphalerite and the rest) does not smelt until roasted: in a firepit, on any fuel at about {{roastPoint}} °C, one at a time, {{firepit}} % of its metal comes out in the roasted concentrate; the reverberatory roaster {{reverberatory.keeps}}. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreroasting\">Roasting sulfide ore</a> and <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-amalgam-handbook-text": "Free gold or silver caught in mercury, on the amalgam pan or the amalgamation plates. It does not smelt: retort it in the still (a boiler on a lit firepit with a condenser beside it), which gives back {{mercuryReturn}} of the mercury and leaves the gold or silver as sponge (<a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreretorting\">Retorting mercury</a>). See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-litharge-handbook-text": "Lead oxide: the lead a cupel or the cupellation furnace burns off its charge. It smelts back to lead at a small loss, {{litharge.out}} to every {{litharge.in}}. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-bullion-handbook-text": "Base bullion: what an ore with a second metal smelts to, a bar of {{barUnits}} units of its metal with the second metal still in it. A parting machine wins the second metal: the cupellation furnace silver-lead, argentiferous copper and cupriferous silver (plain galena's silver-lead can go through the Parkes kettle first), the liquation furnace tin-lead, and the acid parting vessel doré. Smelted again as it is, a bar gives its metal (doré {{unparted.quartz_nativegold}} % of its gold) and the second metal is lost. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>.",
  "oreproduct-richlead-handbook-text": "Lead carrying the silver a Parkes kettle took out of plain galena's silver-lead, {{richLeadSilver}} of its lead. The cupellation furnace parts all of the silver from it. Smelted again as it is, it gives lead, and the silver is lost. See <a href=\"handbook://seraphhorizons-oreprocessing\">Ore processing</a>."
}
